 {:seon.task/name "a-process-catches-up-to-a-db-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-07, of ops passed by hand to each consumer: \"aren't the ops hacks\". Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: consumers are handed each commit's ops: root's writer (writer.clj:45) and the fork server's :tx (fork_server.clj:197) run load-changes!(db, ops); the fork server carries the text index by ops (doc/carry-index!, fork_server.clj:189); merge-main! loaded by ops (agent.clj:587 at HEAD; the uncommitted runtime change moves it to re-fork plus load-diff!). Tried and reverted (docs/sharing.md#catching-up-to-a-db-value): a diff cost 1.8M ticks on a one-datom write where the report is free. Cause: each consumer applies a report and none holds the value it last applied, so a missed report (merge-main!'s base-commit write left the text index behind) is never seen. Design (one derivation's program reaction, ruling 2026-11-14): each consumer holds the reference it last applied ({:branch :commit :t}, as an agent's :program now does); a move is the reaction from that reference to the new one: the report when it starts at the held value (the common case, free), else diff-plan between the two values (the cost of the diff). One function for root, the fork server and agents. Deletes: load-changes! and ops passed by hand. Estimate: common case O(report); a gap O(differing datoms). Proof, red first: seon.program-test/after-head-moves-transact-merge-restart-loaded-vars (a transact, a merge and a restart leave the same loaded vars, registry, config and index)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/after-head-moves-transact-merge-restart-loaded-vars}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-namespace-defining-a-var-twice-is-red"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-10 (Context's 2b, reported by the managing session): Context's merge bea0f837d6 left two defns of seon.render.ui/agent-parts in src/seon/render/ui.clj; the later one silently redefined Web's, 36 tests went red, and no check was red for the cause. The class: a namespace that defines the same var twice passes every check, against AGENTS.md section 5 (\"Every definition happens once, where it is defined\"). What allowed it: the program import records each form's definitions (:form/defines) and a var's one :var/form, the last writer winning, and nothing compares a namespace's forms' definitions with each other. What correct looks like: an import (seon.program/record-tx and the seed's tx-of) that meets a second live form in one namespace defining a var another live form of that namespace defines is a problem of the namespace, red in bin/check health and refused at a merge, read from the import's facts (:form/defines per namespace), never a regex over source text. A form that replaces another (the same form edited, a defn moved within the file) is not a second definition: the old form is no longer live. Sweep on main f4968a8624: 0 duplicate top-level definitions in src and test (a count by a text scan, used only to size this; the check itself reads facts). Proof, red first: a world (my.test/world) with a namespace of two forms defining one var is red in the program's problems; one form, green. Merged from seon.source/a-var-defined-twice-in-a-file-is-said (2026-11-10 triage): same cause (a namespace or file defining a var twice passes every check); that task gains that both import paths (whole import and refresh-tx) keep the same one live form, the last by file order"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/world-namespace-has-two-live-forms-defining-var}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-reach-follows-the-components-its-render-calls"
  :seon.task/plan "Read 2026-11-10 (Web's case: a mutation of seon.render.ui/agent-parts reused the green of seon.ctx-test/a-join-added-to-the-agents-pattern-is-a-part-of-its-page; forced, it went red): a var literal in hiccup (#'agent-parts) IS a reference (checked: :form/refs holds it). The test reaches its page's render through seon.db/entity-index, which picks the entity schema at run time; a registry walker takes the schema files' text (:seon.env/schemas), never the code of the renders those entries name. Correct, first: a registry walker's form takes edges to every render a schema entry names (the schema rows' :refs vars), so its tests take the renders' code; cost: a render edit reruns the ~1,160 tests taking the schema class until recorded reads narrow it. Earlier finding: Found 2026-11-09 by Context in jolt/erase-window: seon.render-cost-test/a-restart-draws-its-turns-in-one-walk and drawing-latest-costs-one-request kept one digest each (d7e7e745f410e858, 4eb46df4e8d68238) across a change to seon.ctx/placed-messages, which the walk reaches at run time through a component var a render holds as data (the page's hiccup names components by var), so a red and a later green sat at one digest and read as flaky. Same class as the process entry (fixed 2026-11-09 by adding seon.repl/serve! to :seon.config.source/process-entry): a reach the static graph misses. Correct: the reach graph follows the component vars a render's hiccup or pattern names (a quoted var or symbol in head position of a component vector, as seon.render reads them), so a test that walks a page reaches what its components call. Never delete a red verdict to clear it (owner, 2026-11-09). Proof, red first: a test walking a page whose component calls f moves digest when f changes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/test-walking-page-hiccup-names-component-var-calls}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-verdict-holds-the-schema-entries-it-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Recorded reads for in-process tests (owner, 2026-11-10: build it). What exists: a test's digest is static, its reach plus the input classes its reached forms name (seon.program/graph-index, env-class-readers). seon.db/entity-index chooses an entity schema at run time by the attributes an entity holds, so no static name says which entity schemas a test reads; since 19076dfbbe every test reaching it takes the whole :seon.env/schemas class: about 1,160 of 1,614 tests rerun on any entity-schema edit (599 before), and 196 of 757 landings from 2026-10-27 to 2026-11-10 touched the schema resources. The gate: (1) ours: render already records what a component read and keeps it per component (seon.render's :reads, d/reads-index, the readers index a transaction is matched against); verdicts are files keyed by digest (seon.fx/write-verdicts!), reused by seon.proofs/plan. (2) v1 selected by static reach only (seon.test, test_reaching_test), with no run-time record. (3) The field: Bazel's unused_inputs_list (an action names the inputs it did not read, and the next key drops them), ninja's depfiles (the compiler writes the headers it read; the next build keys on them), Salsa (each query records the queries it read and is revalidated by their revisions). (4) It adds one field to a verdict file, :reads {schema key content digest}, and a second reuse step; the static digest cannot hold it because the choice is made at run time. The recorder exists: seon.ratom's tracking view (defview: ILookup, IFn, Associative, Seqable over a value, each read notified by its key path, a missing key as a :shallow read, an iteration as a :keys read of its path), which is how a render knows what it read. Its readers (surveyed 2026-11-10) read the entity index by key, (es a), (:schema-identities ix) of a schema, required-keys of a touched schema, except seon.db/reaching (iterates :targets) and the write check's cascade (keys of :identity-schemas, only on a whole-entity retraction): those record a :keys read, which reads every entry, honestly. Plan: in a test child, the entity index each reader takes (seon.db/index, index-of) is that view over the value, the test run in a context that collects the reads, so each schema key resolved and each MISS (a key no schema holds) is recorded at the read, never at a cache fill (the second of two tests in one process reads through the first's cache). The verdict gains :reads. For an in-process test the digest drops :seon.env/schemas (the class of 19076dfbbe), and a verdict at that digest is reused only when every recorded key's current form digest equals the recorded one and no recorded miss now matches a schema; a verdict with no :reads (written before) reruns once. Process tests keep the class: their reads happen in other processes. Proved at n = 3 (2026-11-10, a scratch test, the binary's index swapped for a view and each test run in a context collecting reads): seon.problems-test/a-root-on-a-stale-binary-is-a-problem read 53 index paths, all by key (an attribute to its owning schema: a handful of entity schemas); seon.task-test/deleting-a-cited-var-is-refused-naming-its-cites read 54 by key plus the whole of :identity-schemas (the cascade on a whole-entity retraction, as surveyed); seon.problems-test/health-reads-each-part-once read none of the binary's index (it takes another: find which before building). The recorded path [:entity-schemas a] maps to the schema owning a, [:required-keys s] and [:forms s] to s, a :keys read of a top-level map to every entry. Estimates to measure before building past the recorder (stop if any is over 2x): tests moved by an edit of :seon.cite/cite-fact 150-250, of the agent's fact 300-500, of the eval's fact 250-400 (in-process only), against about 1,160 today. Cost line: the recorder is one set conj per lookup (n = lookups a test makes); the reuse check is one form-digest compare per recorded key, read from the schema entries once per plan. Proofs, red first: a test that reads only app.calc's forms is reused when an unrelated entity schema changes and reruns when a schema it matched changes; two tests in one process both record a schema the first one's lookup cached; a recorded miss reruns when a new schema matches it; a verdict without :reads reruns once. FOUND BY THE RECORDER (jolt/recorder f19af3b8ef, not landed, 2026-11-10): the index recorder works (read sets small: median 0-4 entity schemas a test over problems-, task-, db-, render- and ctx-test; a recorded lookup 345 ticks against 75-125), but (1) tests read the entity schemas OUTSIDE the index: Datahike's schema derived from them (seon.db/schema, under d/db-with and ts/test-db) and the contract registry (contract/properties); those tests record nothing, so a digest without the schema class on the strength of index reads alone lets a schema change through on a reused green, the false green this task exists to close; (2) a view read is two contracted calls, so call-count cost proofs go red under recording; (3) a value cached once per process (my.test/world) gives its reads only to its first taker. RE-PLAN (the managing session's go, 2026-11-10): record at the one choke point every reader of a schema form passes (seon.contract's lookup of a form in the registry), the db schema's derivation recorded as a read of the entity schemas it derives from; seon.cost measures with recording unbound; a cache keeps the reads it was filled with and replays them to each taker. THE RISK THAT DECIDES WHETHER TO BUILD IT: the derived Datahike schema is built from every entity schema, so if every test that transacts reads the whole derived schema, its read set is again every schema and the gain is gone. Then what to record is which attributes' schema a transaction actually consults (the attributes of its datoms, checked one by one), not the derivation. Before more is built: re-measure the read sets with the registry recorder on the same five namespaces, and count the tests that record every schema; if most do, record per attribute a transaction consults, or stop and say so. Wiki: docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache and docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach. Merged from seon.program/the-entity-index-readers-take-only-the-entity-schemas-that-can-match (2026-11-10 triage): same cause (the coarse :seon.env/schemas class on every test reaching the entity index); the recorded-reads task already states the finer sound input, so it gains the measurement-per-landing of class rule against finer rule and the proof that a test naming one entity's attributes keeps its digest under an unrelated schema edit"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/test-reads-only-app-calc-s-forms-reused}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-resumed-agent-binds-every-var-its-program-holds"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Found 2026-11-11 by data (reproduction on jolt/inspect ab2f0fa5cf, not chased): after bin/system stop and start, a resumed agent had #'my.schema/uses interned and UNBOUND while its siblings imported the same way were bound; (:load-errors @seon.agent/state) was []; uses was the last form of its file and the only one whose definition did not change in the last import. What exists [M]: a resumed agent loads load-diff! from its base to its branch (agent.clj:2205 at HEAD); the fork server loaded main at its boot by load-all! (fork_server.clj:354), which skips a form held? by the binary (program.clj:1380-1393). Cause [H]: the same class as seon.program/loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound: a var unmapped by one step (an import's retract) and judged held by another (held? by form id), so neither loads it; an unbound var with no load error is a silent miss. Design: under the one start, load-diff! from the binary's recorded forms to the reference's db, and a check at the end of the start (bootstrap, once): every var the reference's program defines is bound in this process, else the start answers a load error naming it (principle 5: the check inside the one owner). Estimate: one bound? per defined var at start, O(program vars) once (~5,700 lookups). Proof, red first: seon.program-test/a-resumed-agent-binds-every-var-its-program-holds (a store whose last import changed every form of a namespace but its last; a resumed agent has that var bound)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-resumed-agent-binds-every-var-its-program-holds}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-loaded-in-place-unbinds-what-it-deletes"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Seen 2026-11-10 by web on the standing root: a landing that deleted seon.render.ui/users-side left (resolve 'seon.render.ui/users-side) a var and root's page drew both agent lists; a restart drew one. What exists [M]: load-changes! (program.clj:1514) now unmaps each var whose pointer the ops retract and do not re-add (remove-definition!, :1269, ns-unmap, at :1530-1534); so the var is no longer resolvable after a commit that retracts it [H: not re-run]. Left [H]: a caller compiled against the old Var still holds the Var object and calls it (ns-unmap removes the name, not the object), so live code reaches deleted definitions until its caller is reloaded. Cause: an in-place load of root's own program diverges from a boot of the merged tree. Design (one derivation): root's program follows main by the program reaction (load-diff! from its held reference), which removes the deleted var and reloads the live forms whose :form/refs name it (one AVET seek per removed var), so each caller either compiles against the new program or answers its load error, as a boot of the merged tree would; what cannot be made equal in place answers :restart. Estimate: O(changed forms + callers of removed vars). Proof, red first: seon.program-test/a-merge-loaded-in-place-unbinds-a-deleted-var (a merge deleting f whose caller g is unchanged: after the load, g no longer reaches the old f, and its load error names f, as a boot's would)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-merge-loaded-in-place-unbinds-a-deleted-var}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-reads-the-checkout-only-through-a-classed-reader"
  :seon.task/plan "Point's ruling 2026-11-11, on the docs reach (a 4-line wiki edit reran 117 tests): build only if the checkout-path rule shows its edge. What exists after that rule: a raw reader (```seon.program/tree-readers```: slurp, file-seq, io/resource) puts its test on the whole tree only in a form that names the checkout (seon.config/checkout) or passes a relative path; the wiki's and the task files' readers take their own file classes (```seon.program/env-class-readers```). Its unsound edge: a checkout path built in a helper the form calls without naming the checkout reads the checkout with no digest of it, which only the full run catches. Correct when that edge shows (a test reused over an edit to a file it read): a law that refuses a raw read of the checkout outside the classed readers (seon.doc/pages, seon.system/task-files, seon.fx/read-source), from the compiler's facts as the catch law reads them, so every checkout read has a class and its digest. Proof: a test form that slurps a checkout file through a helper is a problem row naming it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-raw-read-of-the-checkout-is-a-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-proofs-namespace-stays-a-test"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 by the Tests session (finding 12's sweep, jolt/tests-agentproof). An agent's namespace made at its REPL gets a file by its name (```seon.source/namespace-path```: a -test suffix goes under test/, anything else under src/), so a proofs-only namespace like lab.proofs is written back under src/, root loads it after a merge (```seon.program/test-nss```, loadable), and ```seon.program/program-names``` counts its test vars as program in a task's write set. The catch law now tells a test by its :test fact (:var/meta), not its file. Correct: the same fact decides the rest: a namespace whose every var is a deftest is a test namespace wherever its file is, written under test/ and never loaded by root. Proof: an agent's lab.proofs with only deftests is written under test/ and not in program-names."
