 {:seon.task/name "a-forms-refs-are-what-its-author-wrote"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 working a task as an agent: a function is its own caller. Measured the same night on main, 5,685 live forms: :form/refs holds 124,881 names; 29,368 of them name Seon's, Datahike's, malli's or Jolt's vars and 95,513 name clojure and host names; of the 29,368, 4,778 (16.3%) are not in their form's own text, nearly all of them two names the contract macro expands to (seon.contract/on? in 2,328 forms, seon.contract/checked in 2,298); and 4,159 forms (73%) refer to their own name. What it does not cost: a prove's reach. The expansion adds refs from a contracted function to two vars of seon.contract, and reach runs from a change to the tests that reach it, so an ordinary change reaches the same tests with refs as written; a change to those two vars reaches everything, which is true. What it costs: a cited function is listed among its own callers, so its source is in an agent's pull and on its page twice. Plan, small: where a var's callers are read, the form that defines the var is not one of them (or the var's own name is left out of its form's refs where they are written: read both sites and pick the one with fewer readers to check). A question to answer before anything else is touched: who reads the 95,513 names of clojure and the host, three quarters of every program's refs, stored and indexed; read so far, in seon.program alone: three checks look for exact names among them (clojure.core/eval, clojure.java.io/resource, and the banned names); no reader of the rest was found there, and the laws and the other namespaces are not read yet. Proof, red first: on the world, a contracted function is not among its own callers; a prove's selection for a changed contracted function is unchanged."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-forms-refs-hold-what-its-text-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-file-defining-one-var-twice-is-a-fact-of-its-import"
  :seon.task/plan "Follow-up of bin/laws defs (jolt/tests 00f1a8ea49), root's ask: the law should query the store's forms, the Seon way, not read text, and so have no blind spot for a definition whose ^{...} spans lines. What exists [M, tests]: the import keeps one form per var: a later form redefining a var supersedes and retracts the earlier (seon.program/form-tx, program.clj:696), so after an import the store cannot show that a file defined a name twice. Plan: the import of a file whose forms define one var twice records it (a :seon.problems class, its file and var, or the import's refusal as the writer refuses a bad config), and bin/laws defs then reads that instead of the text, deleting its perl scan. Owner: the import's (data or runtime); tests reviews the law's side."
  :seon.task/kind :work
